docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:3:The update lifecycle is explicit and operator-controlled:
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:5:1. `update entity-sources rebuild` derives installed update sites from subject manifests, installed subjects, registries, and overrides.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:6:2. `update candidates refresh` fetches local/remote manifests, compares installed and available versions, writes `runtime/update/candidates.json`, and creates notifications.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:7:3. `update changelog show` prints the candidate changelog URL and local changelog content for file-based manifests.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:8:4. `update stage` copies or downloads the artifact into `runtime/update/staged/<candidate-id>/` and verifies sha256 when required.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:9:5. `update verify` checks the staged artifact checksum and, for package zip artifacts, validates subject id/type/version from the package manifest.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:10:6. `update apply --confirm` creates a backup, applies the local-file update for supported subject types, updates installed subjects, and writes an apply record.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:11:7. `update rollback` restores the backup and writes a rollback record.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:12:8. `update doctor` validates update runtime caches.
docs\concepts\update-lifecycle.md:14:`apply` never runs automatically. Tool updates with `custom_command_requires_confirmation` are blocked by default; ProcessForge does not execute arbitrary remote postinstall scripts.
